Apply a bounded one-shot retry around the WaitAsync call to convert the ObjectDisposedException race into the documented "observationally equivalent to a cache miss" behavior the P15-012/P15-055 comment block already promises. ~10 LOC in LacerteSessionManager.cs:1931-1948 plus one regression test.

CODE CHANGE (LacerteSessionManager.cs ExecuteWithIdempotencyLock):

```csharp
private async Task<T> ExecuteWithIdempotencyLock<T>(string? key, Func<Task<T>> body)
{
    if (body == null) throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(body));
    if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(key))
    {
        return await body().ConfigureAwait(false);
    }

    // P18-NEW — bounded retry: EvictIdempotencyEntry can Dispose the
    // semaphore between our GetOrAdd and our WaitAsync (CurrentCount==1
    // is true during that window). On the first ODE, re-GetOrAdd (which
    // installs a fresh semaphore if the prior was evicted) and try once
    // more. If THAT one also disposes mid-window, fall through to the
    // body without locking — observationally equivalent to a cache miss,
    // which is the documented worst-case behavior of EvictIdempotencyEntry.
    SemaphoreSlim? sem = null;
    for (int attempt = 0; attempt < 2; attempt++)
    {
        sem = _idempotencyLocks.GetOrAdd(key!, _ => new SemaphoreSlim(1, 1));
        try
        {
            await sem.WaitAsync().ConfigureAwait(false);
            break;
        }
        catch (ObjectDisposedException)
        {
            sem = null;
            // loop to re-GetOrAdd a fresh semaphore
        }
    }

    if (sem == null)
    {
        // Two consecutive evictions inside the GetOrAdd→WaitAsync window
        // is statistically negligible but theoretically possible; degrade
        // gracefully to unlocked execution (cache-miss equivalent).
        return await body().ConfigureAwait(false);
    }

    try
    {
        return await body().ConfigureAwait(false);
    }
    finally
    {
        try { sem.Release(); }
        catch (ObjectDisposedException) { /* evictor disposed it while
            we held it — Release on a disposed sem is a no-op semantically;
            swallow, since the body already ran and the caller's contract
            is met. */ }
    }
}
```

UPDATE THE COMMENT BLOCK (lines 1923-1930) to reflect the actual invariant:
"P15-012 / P15-055 / P18-NEW — lock entries are opportunistically evicted alongside cache entries by EvictIdempotencyEntry. Two races exist and are both handled: (1) a new GetOrAdd installing a fresh sem before TryRemove — the atomic KVP-pair TryRemove makes the eviction a no-op; (2) a pre-existing reference held by a thread that hasn't yet entered WaitAsync — sem.Dispose() makes the subsequent WaitAsync throw ObjectDisposedException, caught here with a bounded one-shot retry. Worst case after both retries fail is one execution of the body without serialization — observationally equivalent to a cache miss."

REGRESSION TEST (src/LacertMCP.Tests/Session/IdempotencyLockDisposeRaceTests.cs):
- Seed `_idempotencyLocks` and `_idempotencyCache` (via reflection on the private fields, mirroring whatever pattern P15-012's cap test uses) to (IdempotencyCacheMaxEntries - 1) entries.
- Launch 32 parallel ExecuteWithIdempotencyLock calls with the SAME key.
- Concurrently, from a 33rd thread, repeatedly call EvictIdempotencyEntry(key) (via reflection) in a tight loop.
- Assert: no ObjectDisposedException surfaces; the body runs at least once and at most (32 + retry-count) times; the test passes deterministically across 100 iterations.
- Without the fix, the test reliably surfaces ObjectDisposedException within 100 iterations on a multicore machine.

REJECTED ALTERNATIVES (and why):
- "Skip Dispose entirely" (option b): tempting but reopens the bounded-managed-memory invariant P15-012 added. The whole reason EvictIdempotencyEntry calls Dispose is to release the kernel-handle-less but managed-memory-real ~200B per semaphore at the 10k cap. Bounded retry preserves the invariant.
- "Refcount + dispose-when-zero" (option c): heavier, requires reasoning about acquire/release across the body, and doesn't actually fix the race — a refcount increment between GetOrAdd and WaitAsync still has the same window.
- Lock-the-eviction-against-the-acquisition with a shared lock: defeats the purpose of per-key SemaphoreSlim by reintroducing a global lock.

Bounded one-shot retry is the smallest fix that preserves the P15-012 invariant, matches the documented worst-case behavior, and is testable sim-only off-Windows.

