```csharp
private async Task<T> ExecuteWithIdempotencyLock<T>(string? key, Func<Task<T>> body)
{
    if (body == null) throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(body));
    if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(key))
    {
        return await body().ConfigureAwait(false);
    }
    // ROUND-5 — between GetOrAdd and WaitAsync, EvictIdempotencyEntry may
    // dispose this exact semaphore (it sees CurrentCount==1 because we
    // haven't entered yet). On ObjectDisposedException, re-acquire via
    // GetOrAdd — the eviction batch already finished considering this
    // key for the current pass, so a fresh semaphore installs cleanly
    // and the retry converges in at most one extra attempt.
    for (int attempt = 0; ; attempt++)
    {
        var sem = _idempotencyLocks.GetOrAdd(key!, _ => new SemaphoreSlim(1, 1));
        try
        {
            await sem.WaitAsync().ConfigureAwait(false);
        }
        catch (ObjectDisposedException) when (attempt == 0)
        {
            continue;
        }
        try
        {
            return await body().ConfigureAwait(false);
        }
        finally
        {
            try { sem.Release(); } catch (ObjectDisposedException) { /* eviction won — body already ran, no retry needed */ }
        }
    }
}
```

The Release-side ObjectDisposedException catch handles the symmetric race where eviction wins between WaitAsync completing and the finally running (rare but possible if WaitAsync raced eviction by enough nanoseconds to slip through pre-dispose CurrentCount check — defense in depth, no behavioural change since body already ran).

